Give desktop the Aerium new tab page.

The same speed dial the Android build draws in native views, as chrome://aerium-newtab,
made the default new tab page.

Native views were right on Android because the new tab page there is native and
a WebUI one would have added a renderer to a screen shown on every tab. That
argument does not carry over: on desktop the new tab page is a page whatever we
do, so there is nothing to save by avoiding one, and the design is expressed in
HTML where it was drawn.

Header-only, matching aerium_patches.h beside it - a DefaultWebUIConfig plus an
inline URLDataSource needs no BUILD.gn entry, no .cc and no TypeScript.

HOW IT BECOMES THE NEW TAB PAGE. HandleNewTabPageLocationOverride already reads
prefs::kNewTabPageLocationOverride and, with ungoogled-chromium's
add-flag-for-custom-ntp patch, the --custom-ntp switch. This adds one fallback
AFTER both of those, so it fills an empty answer and never displaces one:
an enterprise NewTabPageLocation policy still wins, and so does the flag. The
incognito guard above is untouched, so incognito keeps its own new tab page.

WHERE THE SHORTCUTS LIVE. A profile pref, written through two WebUI message
handlers. localStorage on a chrome:// origin would have been less code and was
not used: whether it survives there is not something this project has checked,
and a speed dial that silently forgets itself is worse than one that cost twenty
more lines. The page is the only writer and the only reader, and it parses what
comes back inside a try, so a value hand-edited into nonsense costs an empty
grid and nothing else. A shortcut is normalised to http or https before it is
stored, the same rule the Android grid applies.

NO SEARCH BOX, deliberately, and this is a difference from the Android screen
rather than an omission. There the address bar can sit at the bottom and the new
tab page is the reliable place to start typing. On desktop the omnibox is on
screen already, and a second field that cannot focus the first without a mojo
round trip is decoration.

The pref names are spelled out in browser_prefs.cc and
chrome_content_browser_client.cc rather than included from the WebUI header,
which is what aerium.drm_enabled already does and the reason is the same: the
alternative is pulling a WebUI header into the prefs file and into the content
browser client. Both sites name the header they must match.

Verified before committing, against the real 152.0.7977.82 sources. The three
edited files were first brought to the state the series leaves them in -
ungoogled-chromium's add-flag-for-custom-ntp for the override function, and its
disable-ai and first-run-page plus our own aerium-patches-page for the WebUI
config list - because a hunk generated against pristine would not have matched a
tree those patches had already touched. Every #include in the new header was
checked to exist at that tag, the raw string holding the page was checked for a
premature terminator, and the finished patch was re-applied to a clean copy of
that baseline: it lands with no fuzz and reproduces all four files byte for
byte, checked with diff.

Not done yet: a settings screen for it, rows and columns controls, drag to
reorder, editing a shortcut in place, and the background chooser. The grid is
fixed at four columns here.

SETTINGS AND BACKGROUNDS, added after the first version of this patch.

The settings live on the page, behind a gear, rather than in chrome://settings.
That is a real difference from the Android build, which got a proper Settings
screen, and it is deliberate twice over: a desktop settings section means the
TypeScript, Polymer and BUILD.gn resource pipeline this whole header-only shape
exists to avoid, and Chrome's own new tab page customises itself from the page
for the same reason. The panel covers background, shortcuts per row, the
bookmarks row, and clearing the grid.

The backgrounds are DRAWN, not photographs, and that is the one thing here that
is a placeholder rather than a decision. Shipping real photos means shipping
image files, and there are none in the tree; the sets below are CSS gradients in
four moods, four each, so the chooser behaves exactly as it will with photographs
and costs nothing on disk. Dropping real images in later changes the SETS table
and nothing else. If you want photographs, they need to arrive as files.

Settings ride in one JSON string for the same reason the shortcuts do: the page
is the only reader and writer, so a pref per checkbox would be four more
registrations and four more handlers to say the same thing. An unparsable value
falls back to the defaults in the page rather than failing.

Verified the same way as the rest, plus two checks the page itself earned: its
script was extracted and run through node --check, and every getElementById
target in it was cross-checked against the ids in the markup - both clean. The
raw string was re-checked for a premature terminator after the additions.

DRAG TO REORDER, and the base::ListValue correction.

Reordering is a drag within the grid. The cells are anchors, so the browser
would drag the link by default; setting the payload in dragstart replaces that
with a move.

The handlers take const base::ListValue&, not const base::Value::List&. That is
not a style choice: content/public/browser/web_ui.h in this tree declares
MessageCallback as RepeatingCallback<void(const base::ListValue&)>, and
base::ListValue is a top-level class in base/values.h here rather than a nested
Value::List. The first version of this patch used the nested spelling and
pre-flight 36 rejected it four times over, once per handler:

    aerium_newtab.h:425:45: error: no type named 'List' in 'base::Value'

The element API is identical - empty(), size(), operator[] - so only the
parameter type changed and every handler body is untouched. The reason is
written next to the first handler so it does not get "corrected" back.

This is a variant difference rather than an upstream API I misremembered, and it
was visible earlier today: chrome_browsing_data_lifetime_manager.cc in this same
tree has GetOriginTypeMask(const base::ListValue&), which I read this morning
and did not follow up. Checking the actual declaration in web_ui.h would have
cost one fetch.
